Let $m\ge200$ be an integer, put $h=m^{-3/5}$, and let $u=w_m^{(r)}$ for either $r=1$ or $r=2$. Write $\bar u(s)=\int_0^1u(s,v)\,dv$. Uniformly in $s$, the supremum plus total variation of $v\mapsto u(s,v)$ is $O(m^{13/5})$, hence $O(m^{10})$, and $$\label{eq:kernel-estimates}
 \begin{aligned}
 &\|\bar u\|_\infty\le2,\qquad
   \int_0^1\bar u=1-2m^{-2},\\
 &(1-2m^{-2})^2\le\int_0^1\bar u^2
       \le1+\frac{h^2}{(1-h)^2},\qquad
   \iint_{[0,1]^2}u^2=\frac2h\log\frac{m^2}{2}.
 \end{aligned}$$*




*For the sampling estimate let $0<N_0\le N\le m^2$, $Q=m^{(7/10)m}$ and $H=m^{300}$. Let $\lambda$ be uniform on $[1/N_0,2/N_0]$. If $n\in[Q/2,Q]\cap\mathbb N$, the density $p_n$ of $\{n\lambda\}$ satisfies $$\label{eq:scalar-marginal}
 |p_n-1|\le N_0/n\quad\hbox{almost everywhere}.$$ If two distinct such powers $n,n'$ satisfy $$|vn+v'n'|>Nm^{1000}
 \quad(0<|v|+|v'|,\ |v|,|v'|\le H,\ v,v'\in\mathbb Z),$$ then, for intervals $I,J\subset[0,1)$, $$\label{eq:scalar-rectangles}
 \left|\mathbb P(\{n\lambda\}\in I,\{n'\lambda\}\in J)
             -|I||J|\right|=O(m^{-80}).$$ Consequently, for complex functions $f,g$ of bounded variation on $[0,1]$, with $B(f)=\|f\|_\infty+\operatorname{Var}(f)$, $$\label{eq:scalar-bv}
 \left|\mathbb E[f(\{n\lambda\})g(\{n'\lambda\})]
           -\left(\int_0^1f\right)\left(\int_0^1g\right)\right|
       \le C m^{-80}B(f)B(g).$$ All implicit constants are absolute, for sufficiently large $m$, and uniform in $N_0,N,n,n'$ and the intervals or functions.

For the fixed $\theta$ there are positive integers $m_j,K_j,G_j,q_i$, positive real numbers $L_j,C_j,\varepsilon_j$, and consecutive blocks $J_j=\{d_{j-1}+1,\ldots,d_j\}$ of size $m_j$, with $d_0=0$, having the following properties. Set $$\begin{gathered}
 q_0=1,\qquad u_i(x)=\{q_ix\},\qquad\alpha_i=\{q_i\theta\},\\
 \kappa_j=(100j)^{-1},\qquad N_j=m_jK_j,\\
 a_j=G_j/m_j,\qquad b_j=m_j^{-19/20},\qquad Q_j=m_j^{(7/10)m_j}.
\end{gathered}$$ The frequencies are strictly increasing and divisible, and $$\label{eq:phases}
 \frac1{N_j}<\alpha_i<\frac2{N_j},\qquad
 q_i\mid q_{i+1},\qquad \frac{q_{i+1}}{q_i}\ge N_j
 \quad(i\in J_j).$$ For every integer $k$ with $N_j/4\le |k|\le m_j^{(4/5)m_j}$, at least $m_j/20$ indices in $J_j$ satisfy $$\label{eq:spread}
 1-\{k\alpha_i\}\in[b_j,1-b_j].$$ If $G_jK_j\le |k|\le N_j/4$, every index in $J_j$ has $1-\{k\alpha_i\}\in[a_j,1-a_j]$.*



*The initial values are $K_1=100,G_1=1$. The numbers $L_j\ge1$ are nondecreasing, $C_j=\max(L_j,K_j^2)$, and the sizes satisfy $$\begin{aligned}
\label{eq:scales}
 &m_j\ge\max(K_j,2^j,4G_j,200),\qquad
 K_{j+1}=\lceil m_j^{(3/4)m_j}\rceil>2K_j,\notag\\
 &G_{j+1}K_{j+1}<m_j^{(4/5)m_j},\qquad
 \kappa_jm_j^{1/40}\ge L_j,\qquad
 \kappa_{j+1}\sqrt{G_{j+1}}\ge L_j,\notag\\
 &h_{j+1}^*/K_{j+1}\le2^{-j}.
\end{aligned}$$ Put $D_j=\exp((K_j+1)\sum_{s<j}C_sm_s)$. If $A,B$ are Laurent polynomials supported in $[-K_j,K_j]$, every coefficient has modulus at most $D_j$, and $|A_0|,|B_0|\ge1/j$, then $$\label{eq:polynomial}
 \max_{0\le n\le2K_j}|(AB)_{-n}|\ge\varepsilon_j,
 \qquad
 (2K_j+1)3e^{-L_j/10000}\le\varepsilon_j/j.$$*


*Finally put $p_*=d_{j-1}$ and $\ell_j=((j+1)^2(1+p_*)q_{p_*})^{-1}$. For every $t=r/q_{p_*}$ with $r\in\mathbb Z$, $0\le r<q_{p_*}$, there is a finite nonempty set $\mathcal N_{j,t}$ of positive integers in $[Q_j/2,Q_j]$, with $\{n\theta-t\}\in(0,\ell_j)$, such that for $r'=1,2$, $$\label{eq:sampling}
 \left\|\frac1{|\mathcal N_{j,t}|}\sum_{n\in\mathcal N_{j,t}}
       \prod_{i\in J_j}w_{m_j}^{(r')}(u_i(x),\{n\alpha_i\})-1
       \right\|_{L^2(\mu)}<\frac1j.$$

Let $p\in M$ be a projection satisfying $(1-p)T_*p=0$. For every $j$, $$\label{eq:coupling-identity}
 (1-p(y))\operatorname{diag}_{k\in\mathbb Z}\bigl(R_{x,y}(k)\bigr)p(x)=0
 \quad\text{for }\mathbb P_j\text{-almost every pair}.$$ Moreover $0<R_{x,y}(k)\le1$ for all $k\in\mathbb Z$ almost surely.

Let $K\ge1$ be an integer, $D\ge a>0$, $c>0$, and $\eta,\delta\ge0$. On a probability space with endpoints $x,y$, let $\xi(x),\gamma(y)$ be measurable vectors in the unit ball of $\ell^2(\mathbb Z)$, and let $F_k$ be positive finite measurable functions of an endpoint, with $F_0=1$. Set $$A_v=\frac{\xi_v(x)}{F_v(x)},\qquad
 B_w=F_{-w}(y)\overline{\gamma_{-w}(y)},\qquad
 R(k)=\frac{F_k(y)}{F_k(x)}.$$ Suppose, almost surely, for $0\le n\le2K$ and every $v\in\mathbb Z$, $$\label{eq:generic-convolution}
 \sum_{v\in\mathbb Z}A_vB_{-n-v}=0,\qquad
 0<\frac{F_{n+v}(y)}{F_v(x)}
       \le\min\{R(v),R(n+v)\}\le1.$$ Let $E$ be an event of probability at least $c$ on which $$|A_v|,|B_v|\le D\quad(|v|\le K),\qquad
 |A_0|,|B_0|\ge a.$$ For each $|k|>K$, suppose there are events $H_k^{(1)},H_k^{(2)}$ with $$\label{eq:generic-tail}
 R(k)\le\eta+\mathbf1_{H_k^{(1)}}+\mathbf1_{H_k^{(2)}}\quad\text{on }E,
 \qquad
 \mathbb P(H_k^{(1)}\mid x)\le\delta,\quad
 \mathbb P(H_k^{(2)}\mid y)\le\delta.$$ The conditional bounds are under the full probability law, before restriction to $E$. If $\varepsilon(K,D,a)$ is the positive constant from Lemma <a href="#lem:polynomial-coefficient" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="lem:polynomial-coefficient">7</a>, then $$\label{eq:generic-obstruction}
 c\varepsilon(K,D,a)\le(2K+1)(\eta+2\sqrt\delta).$$

There is a constant $c_*>0$ such that, for all sufficiently large $j$, $$\label{eq:finite-lower-bound}
 \mathbb P_j(E_j)\ge c_*,\qquad
 \mathbb E_j\left[\mathbf 1_{E_j}
          \sum_{n=0}^{2K_j}|c_{j,n}|\right]\ge c_*\varepsilon_j.$$ The point after which these inequalities hold may depend on $p$.

For every $j$ and every integer $k$ with $|k|>K_j$, there are measurable events $H_k^{(1)},H_k^{(2)}$ in the labelled coupling space such that $$\label{eq:conditional-bad-events}
 \mathbb P_j(H_k^{(1)}\mid x)\le e^{-L_j/400},\qquad
 \mathbb P_j(H_k^{(2)}\mid y)\le e^{-L_j/400}
 \quad\text{almost surely},$$ and on $E_j$, $$\label{eq:tail-ratio-bound}
 R_{x,y}(k)\le e^{-L_j}
       +\mathbf 1_{H_k^{(1)}}+\mathbf 1_{H_k^{(2)}}.$$ The conditional bounds hold before any restriction to $E_j$.

Suppose $p\in M$ is a projection and $(1-p)Tp=0$. For every finite $I\subset\mathbb N$, $$\label{p:eq:pair-identity}
 (1-p(y))\mathop{\mathrm{diag}}_{k\in\mathbb Z}\bigl(R_{x,y}(k)\bigr)p(x)=0
 \quad\text{for }\lambda_I\text{-almost every }(x,y).$$

Put $$\label{p:eq:uniform-product-density}
 H^2=\exp\left(20\left(2\sum_{s\ge1}\kappa_s^2+1\right)\right)<\infty.$$ For every $j\ge1$, the law just constructed has the following properties.*

1.  *Every one-coordinate endpoint density is at most $10$. Both marginals of $\nu_j$ have $L^2(\mu)$ densities of norm at most $H$. The same bound holds for the product law formed by any deterministic choice of the $x$-marginal or the $y$-marginal at each coordinate.*



2.  *Conditional on $x$, the coordinates $y_i$ are independent. Conditional on $y$, the coordinates $x_i$ are independent. If $i\in J_s$, $w=a_s$ or $b_s$, and $u\in[w,1-w]$, then $$\begin{aligned}
      \mathbb P_j(x_i<u\le y_i\mid x)&\ge\rho_s(w)/10
           &&\text{on }\{x_i<u\},\label{p:eq:crossing-given-x}\\
      \mathbb P_j(x_i<u\le y_i\mid y)&\ge\rho_s(w)/10
           &&\text{on }\{y_i\ge u\}.
           \label{p:eq:crossing-given-y}

    \end{aligned}$$ The assertions hold almost surely, simultaneously for any specified countable family of such thresholds.

Let $p\in M$ be an orthogonal projection satisfying $(1-p)Tp=0$ in the product model. For every $j\ge1$, $$\label{p:eq:biased-identity}
 (1-p(y))\operatorname{diag}_{k\in\mathbb Z}\bigl(R_{x,y}(k)\bigr)p(x)=0
 \qquad\text{for }\nu_j\text{-almost every }(x,y).$$ Moreover, $0<R_{x,y}(k)\le1$ for all $k\in\mathbb Z$ almost surely.
